  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nClouds are clearing behind the morning frontal passage, and a \nmostly sunny period is expected during the middle to late \nafternoon period. However, some middle and upper clouds are \nexpected to arrive from the Lower Ohio Valley this evening on \nsouthwest flow aloft. These clouds are expected to be \nthickest/most persistent across far southwest PA, northern WV, \nand southeast Ohio (near/south of I-70) tonight. In these areas,\nthe lack of ideal radiational cooling will help to keep \ntemperatures in the 40s and thus largely negate any frost \npotential. Portions of northwest PA (generally north of US-422) \nand east-central Ohio may clear enough overnight to allow for an\nabbreviated period of radiational cooling. The northwest PA \nportion has the best chance of seeing frost Monday morning, \nespecially in valleys away from rivers, with probabilities of \ntemperatures 36 or colder in the 20 to 50 percent range (these\nmay be a bit low). Potential is high enough for Frost Advisory \nissuance in this area. Chances of freezing temperatures remain \nunder 10 percent, and any freezes will likely be limited to \nisolated colder spots.\n\nConfidence in clear skies is much higher for Monday night as the\nsurface high center moves across the Upper Ohio Valley. This,\nalong with calm wind and lower dewpoints, leads to higher\npotential for frost/freeze conditions, particularly north of \nPittsburgh. Here temps of 36 or less have a 50 to 90 percent \nlikelihood. Freeze potential north of US-422 is in the 10 to 40 \npercent range according to the NBM, but this may be a bit\nunderdone given the ideal setup. Will go with a Freeze Watch for\nthis area, as well as the higher elevations of eastern Tucker\nCounty. A Frost Advisory will likely be needed in other areas as\nwell. This may very well be the last frost/freeze of the season\nwith the rest of the CPC May outlooks favoring warm conditions \nand with no other frosty mornings in the current forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nGenerally dry conditions are forecast from tonight through \nTuesday under high pressure. The lone exception during this \nperiod may be a few showers in Tucker County overnight as \nslightly deeper moisture scrapes that area. \n\nEnsembles are in agreement that the next shortwave trough dips\nacross the Great Lakes Tuesday night and reaches the Upper Ohio\nValley on Wednesday, representing our next chance of widespread\nrainfall. There seems to be only minor differences in\ntiming/depth of the shortwave via cluster analysis, with\ndifferences increasing after the system approaches the Atlantic\nCoast. A wetting rain is highly likely during the period from\nTuesday night into Thursday morning, with most-likely rain\ntotals ranging from 0.3 to 0.5 inch across eastern Ohio, to\naround 0.75 inch north of I-80. Convective/severe chances will\ndepend on the amount of clearing that can occur in the narrow\nwarm sector expected as the system's triple point passes across\nthe lower Great Lakes. Overall, there is low confidence in any\nwidespread severe potential. Extended machine-learning guidance\nfrom NCAR and CSU support this, showing generally modest\nprobabilities in our region.\n\nDry weather returns Thursday night into Friday with transient,\nweak high pressure. Rain chances return next weekend with an\napproaching warm front and moisture increase, as 500mb heights\nbegin to rise and foreshadow a potential pattern flip towards\nabove-normal temperatures.",
